      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core arguments of race realism often hinge on interpretations of genetic studies that purport to show heritable differences in cognitive traits among different populations. Proponents claim that variations in genes linked to intelligence are distributed unevenly across racial groups, suggesting a biological basis for observed differences in average IQ scores. They frequently cite historical or contemporary data, often selectively, to support the idea that these genetic predispositions are fixed and immutable across racial lines. These arguments essentially posit that race is a meaningful biological category that correlates with measurable differences in human potential.",
